    "Welcome to Milestone Project 7! Today marks the architectural culmination of Block 8: SQLAlchemy 2.0 Async ORM and High-Scale Database Patterns. Over the past four days, you have systematically disassembled every critical layer of modern relational data access: asynchronous engine multiplexing, non-blocking connection pool lifecycle mechanics, Unit of Work transactional atomicity, Identity Map entity caching, query plan optimization, N+1 query elimination, and zero-downtime schema evolution. In this milestone project, you transition from theoretical analysis to building an enterprise-grade database tier: a High-Scale Async ORM and Zero-Downtime Data Layer Engine. This comprehensive capstone synthesizes all four database pillars into a unified, production-hardened framework capable of sustaining 10,000 requests per second under strict ACID transactional guarantees across distributed cloud infrastructure.",
    "The first structural pillar of our milestone engine is the Bounded Asynchronous Connection Pool. In high-concurrency microservice architectures, unconstrained database connections represent an existential threat to database availability. If an application opens a new physical TCP socket to PostgreSQL for every incoming HTTP request, sudden traffic surges quickly exhaust database process tables, thrashing memory and degrading query throughput. A production-grade database engine must enforce strict connection bounds through a QueuePool architecture. The pool maintains a fixed set of pre-authenticated, warmed socket connections (pool_size), while allowing a controlled bursting buffer (max_overflow) during peak traffic spikes to absorb transient load without inducing cascading socket failures across backend workers. This prevents runaway thread spawning and protects database CPU resources.",
